WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.660
Health behavior is one of the places where application design and Kubernetes configuration meet directly.

2
00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:10.840
Readiness probes tell Kubernetes whether a Pod should receive traffic.

3
00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.540
Liveness probes help identify a container that should be restarted.

4
00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:20.160
Startup probes can protect slow-starting applications from premature liveness failures while they initialize.

5
00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:23.140
These probes are not decorations in the manifest.

6
00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:29.400
They are signals used by the platform to decide when to send traffic, when to wait, and when to restart.

7
00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:32.400
Alive and ready are different states.

8
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:41.860
A Node.js process can be alive while it is still loading configuration, warming caches, connecting to dependencies, running migrations, or recovering from overload.

9
00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:46.920
A readiness check should fail when the application cannot safely serve traffic.

10
00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:54.700
A liveness check should be narrower and should avoid restarting the process for every temporary downstream dependency failure unless that behavior is intentional.

11
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:57.840
Bad liveness probes can create restart loops.

12
00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:02.340
Bad readiness probes can send users to a Pod that is not prepared to serve them.

13
00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:08.780
Graceful shutdown matters during normal operations, especially rolling updates and node maintenance.

14
00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:14.800
Kubernetes sends SIGTERM and then waits for a configured termination window before forcing the container to stop.

15
00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:27.940
A Node.js application should respond by stopping new work, draining or finishing in-flight requests where appropriate, closing listeners, closing database or queue connections safely, and exiting within the allowed time.

16
00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:35.480
If the process ignores termination, releases can create failed requests, abandoned work, or confusing logs.

17
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:41.760
Lifecycle hooks and termination settings should match the application rather than conceal design problems.

18
00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:48.740
A longer termination grace period can help a service finish in-flight work, but it is not a substitute for signal handling.

19
00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:55.320
A readiness endpoint can protect traffic during startup, but it is not a substitute for fixing broken initialization.

20
00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:58.460
Treat health behavior as part of release engineering.

21
00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:09.200
The safest deployments are those where Kubernetes and the Node.js process agree about when the service is starting, ready, unhealthy, draining, and stopped.
